# p_9f3c — The 21:00 Migration: Kitchen and Couch Items Converge on the Coffee Table

Between 20:00 and 22:30, the household's evening routine pulls objects from the kitchen and the couch onto the coffee table. The evidence: mug_hana is at coffee_table_l1 at 20:00 (×1) and 21:00 (×1) on weekdays; mug_priya is at coffee_table_l1 at 21:00 (×1) and 22:00 (×3) on weekdays and at 21:00 (×1) and 22:00 (×1) on weekends. blanket_shared is at coffee_table_l1 at 21:00 (×2) and 22:00 (×1) on weekdays. snack_bowl_shared is at coffee_table_l1 at 21:00 (×2) on weekdays and 21:00 (×1) on weekends. glass_priya appears at coffee_table_l1 at 20:00 (×1) and 23:00 (×1) on weekdays.

This is the "TV session" convergence: after dinner is cleared from the kitchen table (plates go back to the cupboard around 20:00–20:30), the residents settle on the couch or armchair with their mugs, the blanket, and the snack bowl all on the coffee table in front of them. The kitchen is done; the living room is the social space. This pattern is consistent across both residents and both day types.

What sets this apart: it is the only document that models the *simultaneous* presence of mugs, blanket, and snack bowl on the coffee table in the 20:00–22:30 window. Other documents place the blanket on the couch (p_c9d4), the mugs in the kitchen (p_a9b4), or the snack bowl on the counter (p_b9c4). It differs from p_7d4e which captures the blanket migration but not the mugs, and from p_6a1b which captures mugs but with a different timing window. What would refute it: mug_priya at kitchen_table_k1 at 21:00 on a weekday (the kitchen is still the social space), or blanket_shared at couch_l1 at 21:00 on a weekday (no migration happened).

